        "nav_reading": "Reading",
        "thought_1105": "I said I would try to live a little more happily.\n\nA baseball field in the sun, the drums in a live music venue, occasionally going all the way to a distant lake to watch meteors, eating and drinking with friends, working out, traveling—hours that require no reckoning of gains and losses afterward. Work is work; do it, and that is enough. The rest of the time is there to spend as I please. There are so many things a person can do while alive. Why must we always extract some meaning we can account for to the outside world?\n\nBesides, the world is so large, and full of useless, lovable things.\n\nI have no intention of finding some grand justification for my happiness. Happiness is happiness. It needs no necessary purpose, still less to be converted afterward into some kind of growth.\n\nAnd yet, for more than two years now, whenever I fall asleep, I always think in my dreams that I am still a high school student.\n\nAnd of all places, high school. I hated that place, and still do. Schools in the Northeast are hardly hell on earth; at least, compared with those notoriously oppressive provinces, what I went through is scarcely worth mentioning.\n\nBut what is there to be grateful for? Take the best years of a person’s life, cut them into forty-five-minute pieces, put their freedom, desires, likes, and dislikes under uniform administration—and then, because the guards here happen to be relatively gentle, they are supposed to acknowledge an enormous debt of gratitude.\n\nBut what came afterward?\n\nThe timetable no longer decided my life. Everyone was so very free. Free to come and go, free to drift apart, free to maintain necessary or unnecessary connections in countless chat windows. Mature human society has its elaborate etiquette: everyone understands without saying, and everyone must wish everyone else a brilliant future.\n\nOf course I like it here. I just sometimes wonder whether the freedom we fought so hard to win ever included the right to be close to other people.\n\nBefore, we were forced to be together. Now, no one has to be with you.\n\nAnd loneliness, then—is it freedom’s complimentary gift?\n\nErich Fromm wrote a book called Escape from Freedom. I had known that title for a long time, yet never imagined that escaping freedom could be something I did myself.\n\nHow easily people fall in love with a life arranged for them, even willingly assigning morality to the arrangement. What everyone endures is no longer called oppression; what everyone does need not be questioned as right or wrong. A life that requires no judgment of one’s own can, in time, even be held up as a virtue. The collective gives people dignity, belonging, a reason not to face themselves alone. All one has to do is follow, and for a while one is spared responsibility for oneself.\n\nThe people a crowd never lacks are those who believe they do not belong to it.\n\nThinking of this, I begin to understand why humanity always seems to need a temple, a flag, some beliefs that need not be questioned.\n\nWho would want to face the world alone at every moment?\n\nBut now I no longer dream of high school.\n\nSomething very strange suddenly seems not so strange after all.\n\nI am studying artificial intelligence at Shanghai Jiao Tong University now. It sounds promising. A prestigious university, a good major, some future that seems likely to come true: enough to fool a person into believing they are growing upward. When someone asks, I even feel a little secret vanity.\n\nThat is rather interesting. After all, I am not particularly devoted to worldly achievement.\n\nMy life is actually very good.\n\nYet those few worldly labels still give me peace of mind.\n\nHow very little backbone I have.\n\nI do not want to live by their standards, do not want to compromise my likes and dislikes for them. I even look rather skeptically at lives spent endlessly pursuing success and excellence.\n\nI do not believe, yet I still partake of its incense offerings.\n\nIt seems people are not necessarily very loyal to what they profess to believe.\n\nThis does not make me doubt my life. Those real joys never needed anyone to vouch for them. It is only that things I had long since dismissed in reason have, without my noticing, continued to occupy some part of me. My contempt for them is real, and so is the comfort I take from them. The two even coexisted peacefully, until the change in my dreams made them visible.\n\nIn Homeward Bound, Tao Yuanming writes: “Since I have made my heart serve my body, why be disconsolate and grieve alone?” But when can a person ever know that their heart is not serving something? You can refuse to bow for five pecks of rice, disregard fame and rank, even laugh off other people’s opinions. But what are you to do with the attachments that remain, forced on you by no one and perhaps known to no one?\n\nI don’t know.\n\nPerhaps nothing needs to be done. People are not clear and immaculate things, after all. Our emotions, our desires, the habits we fail to notice: all of them are ourselves.\n\nBut I still dislike it—dislike that even while resisting those things, I quietly ask them for comfort.\n\nI am no longer a high school student.\n\nBut I don’t know whether the university student in my dreams now is a free person, or has once again been lulled to sleep by a few beautiful names.",
        "date_thought_1105": "Nov 5, 2026",
        "moment_date_thought-2026-11-05": "Nov 5, 2026",
